// Generated from README.md - do not edit by hand.
const DOCS_MD = "# VectorGML\n\nA vector editor for game art that runs in the browser and exports to GameMaker.\n\nYou build a design from shapes, lines, curves and named points. Export it and you get one GML script that draws the design with triangles at any size, and that exposes every shape and point as a struct field, so your own code can move, recolor or animate the parts. There is no sprite sheet to regenerate when the art changes.\n\nIt is a single static page: no build step, no dependencies, no server, and nothing is uploaded anywhere.\n\n## Features\n\n- Shapes, custom polygons, B\u00e9zier curves, rounded corners, and lines with dashes, tapered ends and arrowheads\n- Union, subtract, intersect and exclude, plus clipping one shape to another\n- Linear and radial gradients with any number of color stops, outlines, and a shared palette\n- Named points on the design or on a shape, and a pivot for each shape\n- Snapping to a custom grid, guide lines, and the corners and edges of other shapes, with a freehand mode\n- Symmetry drawing, copies in a pattern, reference images, and multi-selection with rotation\n- SVG import, and PNG and SVG export\n- GML export with a triangle count for the cost of each design, and settings for circle and gradient smoothness\n- Undo, version history, and optional saving to a `.design.json` file\n\n## Quick start\n\nOpen `index.html` in a browser. Press **Export GML** in the editor, paste the result into a new script asset in GameMaker, and use it:\n\n```gml\n// Create event\ndesign_player_create(\"hero\");\n\n// Draw event\ndesign_player_draw(\"hero\", x, y);\n```\n\nFunction names are a prefix, the design's name and the action, so a design called `player` gives `design_player_create`, `design_player_draw` and so on. The prefix and naming style can be changed in the export window. Every function is listed under Using a design in GameMaker.\n\nAny current browser works. Linking a design to a file on disk needs Chrome or Edge. Designs are stored in the browser's local storage, so keep copies with Export JSON or Save to file.\n\n## Getting started\n\nDesigns are kept in the browser's local storage, so use the same browser each time. Clearing site data deletes them. For a copy you control, see Saving your work below.\n\nDouble-click a design's name, on the start screen or in the editor header, to rename it.\n\nThe documentation below is also in the editor, under Help > Documentation.\n\n## The editor\n\nThe toolbar is on the left, the canvas in the middle, and two panels on the right: Properties above and Ordering below. Ordering lists the shapes from front to back. The bar along the bottom shows the pointer position, the selection, the grid and zoom controls. Press `?` for the list of shortcuts.\n\n### Tools\n\nPick a tool on the toolbar or press its key. Hover a button for its name. A button with a small corner mark (Move, Shapes) opens a menu of its tools when you click it. After you place something, the tool returns to Move.\n\n- **Move** (`V`) selects, moves and resizes shapes.\n- **Hand** (`H`) drags to pan. Holding Space does the same from any tool.\n- **Shapes** drag on the canvas to draw: `R` square, `O` circle, `3` triangle, `5` pentagon, `6` hexagon, `S` star. Shift keeps the shape square, Alt draws from the center, and a click places it at its default size. The Shapes button lists them all.\n- **Custom polygon** (`P`) is in the Shapes menu and draws a polygon point by point, described below.\n- **Point** (`T`) places a point where you click. Click on a shape to attach the point to that shape.\n- **Line** (`L`) draws an open line, described below.\n- **Color picker** (`I`) copies the color of the shape you click onto the selected shapes.\n- **Symmetry** (Shift+Y, or Symmetry in the status bar) is a switch, not a tool. See Symmetry drawing below.\n\n### Selecting\n\n- Click a shape, or drag across empty canvas to select everything the rectangle touches. Shift-click adds or removes a shape. Ctrl/Cmd+A selects all and Esc deselects.\n- In Ordering, click a row, Shift-click for a range, or Ctrl/Cmd-click to add one. Double-click a row to rename it, or right-click for a menu.\n\n### Moving, resizing and rotating\n\n- Drag a shape to move it. Start the drag with Alt held to move a copy instead. Shift keeps the move to one direction. The arrow keys move one pixel, or ten with Shift held.\n- Pink guide lines show when the edges or center of what you drag line up with another shape or the origin, and the shape snaps to them. Guides in the status bar turns this off. With Snap to the grid off (freehand), a corner point you drag, and each point of a polygon or line you draw, also lines up with the other corners of the same shape: when it comes level with one, or straight above or below one, it snaps to that line and a pink guide shows. Ctrl or Cmd (Alt while drawing a polygon) skips it. (Snapping while drawing a polygon is separate, under Snap.)\n- Drag an edge or corner handle to resize. On a corner, Shift keeps the proportions. Alt resizes from the center. Drag the round handle above the shape to rotate it; Shift snaps to 15 degrees.\n- With several shapes selected, the corner handles scale them together, and the round handle above the selection turns them together around the middle of the selection. Shift snaps to 15 degrees. A shape's own position changes as it swings round, and each shape turns by the same angle.\n- Object > Rotate 90\u00b0 clockwise and counter-clockwise do the same in exact steps. With one shape selected they turn it around its pivot.\n- The Properties panel has exact values for position, size, scale and rotation.\n\n### Editing corners\n\nDouble-click a polygon, or select it and press Enter or choose **Edit Vertex**. Each corner becomes a handle.\n\n- Drag a corner to move it. Shift-click to select several corners and move them together.\n- Switch on **+ Vertex**, then click a side to add a corner there.\n- Delete removes the selected corners. A polygon keeps at least three.\n- The check mark (or Enter) keeps the changes. The cross (or Esc) discards them.\n\nA circle is drawn as a polygon with many sides (see Circles below), but it is edited as a smooth shape. It has two handles, at the top and bottom. Dragging one moves that end, and the circle stretches to fit between the two. Dragging sideways tilts it.\n\n### Rounded corners and smooth lines\n\nSelect a shape and use **Corners** in Properties.\n\n- **Radius** rounds every sharp corner into an arc, in pixels. A radius that is too large for a short side is reduced to fit, so two rounded corners never overlap. The number of segments in each arc comes from the design's Circle sides.\n- **Smooth** turns the line through the corners into a curve, from 0% (straight sides) to 100%. The curve passes through every corner. At high values the curve swings outside the original box.\n- In **Edit Vertex**, select one or more corners to give them a radius or smoothing of their own. **Use the shape's values** removes that override. A corner that is smoothed is not also rounded.\n- **Object > Corners** has quick versions: **Round corners** picks a radius from the shape's size, **Smooth lines** switches smoothing on or off, and **Sharp corners** removes both.\n\nThese work on any shape except a circle, including the result of a boolean operation and combined shapes. The rounded outline is real geometry, so booleans, outlines and gradients treat it like any other shape. A shape made with a boolean operation keeps the rounding the sources had when they were combined, and rounding applied afterwards affects the result as a whole.\n\n### Curves\n\nAny corner can be a curve point with two handles. The side next to a handle becomes a smooth B\u00e9zier curve. The editor cuts the curve into short straight pieces, so the exported script needs nothing new, and the number of pieces follows Circle sides.\n\n- **While drawing**, click and drag to pull out handles. The handle you pull sets one side, and the other one mirrors it.\n- **In Edit Vertex**, select a point to see its handles. Drag a handle to bend the curve, and the other handle turns to stay opposite. Hold Alt while dragging to move one handle on its own. Alt-drag a corner to pull handles out of it. A curved point is drawn round and a corner is drawn square.\n- **Corner and Curved** under Corners in Properties switch the selected points. Curved gives the point handles along the line between its neighbors, the same curve that smoothing makes, ready to edit. Remove curve handles makes the points sharp again.\n- **Object > Corners > Convert to curves** does this for every point of the selected shapes and turns their smoothing into handles you can drag.\n- **+ Vertex** on a curved side splits the curve where you click without changing the shape.\n\nHandles stay the same size in pixels when you move points, and scale with the shape when you resize it. A boolean operation turns curves into plain outlines. A point with handles ignores corner radius and smoothing.\n\n### Lines\n\nChoose **Line** and click to place points. Press Enter, double-click, or click the last point again to finish. Backspace removes the last point and Esc cancels. A line does not close and has no fill.\n\nIn Properties, a line has these settings.\n\n- **Color** and **Opacity**.\n- **Width** is the thickness in pixels.\n- **Smooth** curves the line through its points, from 0% to 100%.\n- **Round ends** or **Flat ends**. Round ends are half circles, like a pen. Flat ends are cut straight across.\n\nEdit Vertex works on lines. Select points to give them their own smoothing, and **+ Vertex** adds a point on the line. A line is flipped, copied and moved like any shape. It cannot be used in a boolean operation or combined.\n\nUnder Line style in Properties a line can also have:\n\n- **Dashes**: a dash length and a gap length in pixels. With round ends and a gap smaller than the width the dashes touch, so use flat ends or a bigger gap.\n- **Taper start** and **taper end**: the line grows from a point at the start, or shrinks to a point at the end, over that percent of its length (100 means half the line each).\n- **Arrow at start** and **arrow at end**, with an **Arrow size** in line widths. The arrowhead is a triangle, and the line stops where it begins.\n\nThese styles can be combined. A styled line is turned into plain filled polygons in the editor, so the canvas, Export image and the exported script all show exactly the same thing, and the game needs no extra code. That also means a styled line is drawn in one solid color, and its circle sides (in the export window) set how round its ends are. Very sharp bends are joined with round corners.\n\n### Custom polygons\n\nChoose the Pen and click to place points. Click the first point to close the outline and fill it. With three or more points placed, Enter also closes it. Backspace removes the last point and Esc cancels.\n\nClick and drag instead of clicking to pull out the point's curve handles, as in other pen tools. The side leading into the point and the side leading out of it both bend toward the handle. Pan with Space or the middle button while drawing. The same works with the Line tool. See Curves.\n\nWhile you draw a polygon or a line, each point goes to the best spot nearby, in this order:\n\n1. A **corner** of any visible shape within a few pixels (a yellow ring). This includes rounded corners, circle points and curve points.\n2. The nearest spot on an **edge** of any visible shape (a yellow diamond), so a shadow can run exactly along a side.\n3. The **grid**, which is whole pixels unless you set another size.\n\nCorners and edges win over the grid. Hold **Alt** to skip all snapping for a point. The **Snap** button in the status bar (or View > Snapping settings) opens Settings: snap to edges and corners on or off, snap to the grid on or off (off means freehand, apart from edges and corners), the width and height of a grid square, and whether the grid is shown. The grid size applies to everything you drag, not just the polygon tools: new shapes, moving, resizing, corner points, pivots and points all land on the grid. With Snap to the grid off, everything is freehand: dragging corner points, moving, resizing and drawing all land exactly where the pointer is (still snapping to corners, edges and guide lines unless those are off too). When the grid is not a whole number of pixels, or is off, the shapes you edit keep their points exactly (like imported shapes), so they stay between pixels.\n\nThe editor does not check whether the outline crosses itself.\n\n### Grouping and combining\n\n**Group** (Ctrl/Cmd+G) keeps the shapes separate but lists them under one name, and selecting or moving one moves all of them. A group can be collapsed, hidden or locked as a unit. Double-click a shape on the canvas to select just that shape and edit its corners. **Ungroup** is Ctrl/Cmd+Shift+G. In GameMaker each group is an array of its shapes.\n\n**Combine** (Ctrl/Cmd+E) turns two or more shapes into one solid shape with a single color, taken from the front-most shape. Circles keep their shape and their number of sides. A combined shape can be moved, rotated, recolored and given points. An edge handle stretches it along one axis and a corner handle scales it evenly. Its parts cannot be edited while combined. **Uncombine** restores the original shapes.\n\n### Symmetry drawing\n\nTurn on **Symmetry** in the status bar, View > Symmetry drawing, or press Shift+Y. A pink dashed line shows the axis, and everything you draw also appears mirrored across it: shapes, custom polygons and lines. While you draw, a faint copy shows where the mirror image will be. Both shapes are selected afterward, so you can recolor or group them together.\n\nThe axis is a vertical line at x = 0, the origin. Change **Symmetry axis** in Properties (select nothing) to move it. A plain shape that is centered on the axis is already symmetric, so no copy is made. Object > Duplicate mirrored makes a mirror image of the selected shapes at any time.\n\nThe two shapes of a pair stay **linked**: move, resize, turn, recolor or reshape either one and the other follows, mirrored across the axis, including while you drag. A small \u21cb in Ordering marks a linked shape, and Properties shows which shape it is linked to. Name, group, points, hidden, locked and clip stay separate, so each can still be named, hidden or given its own points. **Object > Unlink mirror** (or the Unlink button in Properties) turns them into two ordinary shapes. Deleting one leaves the other as an ordinary shape. If you select both and drag, the original decides where the pair ends up. Changing the symmetry axis moves the twin. Copying a shape on its own gives an ordinary shape, and copying a pair together keeps the pair linked. The exported script contains both shapes as plain shapes: the link exists only in the editor (and in the JSON file, as `mirrorOf`).\n\n### Copies in a pattern\n\nSelect one or more shapes and choose **Object > Repeat in a pattern**. The selection is copied as one unit, and the copies appear on the canvas while you adjust the settings in Properties. **Apply** keeps them. **Cancel**, or selecting something else, removes them.\n\n- **Grid** makes rows and columns. Step x, y is the distance between neighbors, center to center.\n- **Line** makes a row along a direction. Each copy can also turn by a number of degrees and be a percentage of the size of the one before, which makes spirals and fans.\n- **Circle** places the copies round a center point, shown as a pink cross. The angle is 360 for all the way round, or less for an arc. By default the copies turn as they go round. Use the origin, or the selection's center, or type a position.\n\nCopies are separate shapes with their own points, and they are grouped with the original unless it is already in a group. At most 500 shapes are added at once. Outlines and line widths keep their size when a copy is scaled.\n\n### Clipping\n\n**Clip to shape** (Object menu) shows a shape only where it lies inside another shape. Select two shapes, and the front one is clipped to the one behind it. It is made for shadows and highlights: draw the shadow bigger than needed, clip it to the object, and it cannot spill over the edge. **Release clip** undoes it. Properties also has a **Clip > Only inside** menu on a single shape.\n\nThe clipped shape keeps its own points, color, gradient and outline, and the cut follows the mask when you move or reshape either one. The outline follows the cut edge. The gradient still spans the whole shape. The exported script contains the cut shape, so the game does no clipping. Circles in the cut use the design's circle sides, not the export window's setting. Lines cannot be clipped or be a mask. Boolean operations and Combine use what is visible after the clip.\n\n### Boolean operations\n\nWith two or more shapes selected, Object > Boolean (or the Boolean button above the canvas) offers four operations. The back-most selected shape is the one the others act on, and the result takes its color and style.\n\n- **Union** merges the shapes into one outline.\n- **Subtract** cuts the other shapes out of the back-most one, leaving holes where they were fully inside.\n- **Intersect** keeps only the area that all the shapes share.\n- **Exclude** keeps the area that belongs to exactly one of the shapes.\n\nThe result is a single shape made of plain polygons, with true holes. It can be moved, rotated, stretched and recolored like a combined shape, but it cannot be split back into the originals, so use Undo for that. Circles are turned into polygons with the design's number of sides first, and the result keeps those sides if the setting changes later.\n\n### Arranging\n\n- The row of buttons at the top of Properties aligns and distributes. Several shapes line up with each other. One shape lines up with the origin, so Align bottom puts its lowest edge on the line y = 0. Distribute needs three shapes and makes the gaps between them equal.\n- Flip horizontal and vertical (Shift+H, Shift+V) mirror the selection. The shapes are mirrored, not scaled by -1.\n- Ordering lists the shapes front to back. Drag rows to reorder them, or use the Arrange buttons. Dropping a shape among a group's shapes adds it to the group.\n- The eye hides a shape and the lock stops the canvas from moving it. Hidden shapes are not drawn in the editor and are exported with `visible` set to false.\n\n### Reference images\n\nA reference image is a picture to trace from. You can add as many as you like. Choose File > Add reference images, press **+ image** under Reference images in Ordering, or drop image files on the canvas. Each appears behind the shapes at half opacity, and only in the editor. They are never exported.\n\nSelect an image in Ordering to change it. Drag it to move it and drag its corner handle to scale it. Properties has its name, exact position and scale, opacity, **Locked** (it cannot be dragged), **Hidden**, and **Over the shapes**, which draws it on top so you can check your work against it. **Forward** and **Backward** change which image is drawn over which. **Replace** swaps the picture and keeps the size and place of the old one. **Delete** removes the image, and so does the trash button on its row or the Delete key. Removing an image can be undone.\n\nThe pictures are stored in this browser, apart from the design. Where each one sits is saved with the design, but the pictures are not part of a `.design.json` file or its version history.\n\n### Color\n\n- **Fill** is solid, linear or radial. A gradient blends from one color to another: a linear one in a direction you set with an angle (0 is left to right, 90 is top to bottom, and it turns with the shape), a radial one from the center outward. Each color has a picker and a hex field, and the color picker tool can fill either. Recently used colors appear below.\n- **Color stops.** A gradient can have up to eight more colors between its two ends. Click the bar under Stops to add one where you click, or press **+ Stop**. Each stop has a position in percent, a color and a delete button. Select a single shape to edit its stops. Stops are not linked to palette colors.\n- **Detail** (under Advanced, radial gradients only) sets how finely this one shape's gradient is drawn in the game, from 1 to 16. Leave it empty to use the default chosen at export. A shape made of a few large triangles, such as a hexagon, shows faceting at low values and needs more than a shape made of many small ones. The cost grows with the square of the value.\n- **Opacity** is a percentage for the whole shape.\n- **Outline** is a width in pixels, 0 for none. The outline grows outward from the shape's edge, with rounded corners, so it does not change the size of the shape. A translucent shape shows its outline through the fill, as it does in the game.\n- The **palette** holds named colors. Select nothing to see it in Properties, or click the + beside the fill color to save the current color. A shape that uses a palette color follows it: change the color once and every shape using it changes. In GameMaker you can change a palette color by name, which is how to make a second version of a design in another color.\n\n### Circles\n\nEvery circle is a polygon with a fixed number of sides, in the editor and in the game, so what you see is what the game draws. Select nothing and change **Circle sides** in Properties, from 8 to 128 in steps of 4. The default is 32. The setting is for the whole design.\n\n### Points\n\nA point is a named position. Add one to the whole design with **+ point** under Design points, to a shape with **+ point** on its row, or with the Point tool.\n\n- A design point is a fixed position in the design.\n- A shape point is stored as an offset from the shape's center, so it moves and rotates with the shape.\n- Select a point and use **Attached to** in Properties to move it onto any shape, or to make it a design point. It stays where it is on the canvas. A selected shape point has a dotted line to the center of its shape.\n\nPoints mark anything your game needs to find, such as where a character holds an item or where something hovers. Both kinds are exported by name, and your code reads them as variables (see Points under Using a design in GameMaker).\n\n### Pivots\n\nA shape turns and scales around its pivot. By default the pivot is the shape's center. Select a shape and use **Pivot** in Properties to move it, press **Move pivot** and drag the purple cross on the canvas, or select one of the shape's points and press **Use as pivot**. Dragging the pivot close to the center puts it back there. Rotating the shape in the editor, with the handle or the Rotation field, turns it around the pivot.\n\nIn the exported script the shape's `x` and `y` are the pivot's position, and the shape's geometry and points are measured from the pivot. So `angle` turns the shape around its pivot, and moving `x` and `y` moves the pivot. Put the pivot on a shoulder, hinge or neck and the shape swings from there.\n\n### The origin\n\nThe origin is the point (0, 0) that your game positions. It is the orange cross on the canvas. Select nothing and use the nine buttons under Origin in Properties to put it on a spot of the design's bounding box, such as the bottom center for a character's feet. You can also drag the cross. Moving the origin slides the design under it, and every position is measured from the origin.\n\n### Settings\n\n**File > Settings** (Ctrl or Cmd and comma) opens every option the editor remembers, in one window. Changes apply at once.\n\n- **Snapping**: snap to corners and edges of other shapes, snap to guide lines, snap to the grid, smart guides, how close the pointer must be to snap (in screen pixels), and the width and height of a grid square.\n- **View**: the grid, rulers and guide lines, point labels, and the game-size preview with its size and background.\n- **Drawing**: symmetry drawing.\n- **Keyboard and rotation**: how far an arrow key nudges, how far Shift plus an arrow nudges, the step for Shift-turning the rotate handle, and the step for the Rotate clockwise and counter-clockwise buttons.\n- **Reset**: put every setting back to its default, or forget the options remembered by the export windows.\n\nSettings are saved in this browser and are not part of a design. The status bar's Grid, Snap, Guides, Symmetry and Preview buttons and the View menu are shortcuts for the same settings. The options for exporting (GML and image) are in their own windows and are remembered there.\n\n### Rulers and guide lines\n\nRulers run along the top and left of the canvas. Drag down from the top ruler for a horizontal guide line, or right from the left ruler for a vertical one. Drag a guide to move it, and drag it back onto its ruler to delete it. Guide lines can also be added and typed in under Guide lines in Properties (select nothing), and View > Rulers and guide lines hides rulers and guides. Guide lines are saved with the design (and in the JSON file as `guideLines`) but are never exported to GameMaker.\n\nThings snap to guide lines: polygon and line points, the edges and center of a shape you move, corner points, and the corners of a new shape. Snap to guide lines in the Snap settings turns this off.\n\n### Snapping to other shapes\n\nMoving a shape, dragging a corner point and drawing a new shape with a shape tool snap the way the polygon tools do: a corner of another shape first (a yellow ring), then the nearest spot on an edge (a yellow diamond), then guide lines, then the grid. A moving shape snaps with any of its own corners. Hold **Ctrl** or **Cmd** to skip the snapping for one drag. (The polygon tools use **Alt** instead, because Alt is how a corner point pulls out a curve handle and how a drag makes a copy.) Snap to edges and corners in the Snap settings turns it off everywhere.\n\n### The grid\n\nShapes, corners and points snap to the grid, and shape edges land on grid lines. The grid is whole pixels by default; the Snap button sets any other size (see Custom polygons). This holds while scale is 1 and the shape is not rotated. Otherwise edges can fall between pixels. View > Pixel grid hides the grid lines and does not change the snapping.\n\n### Preview\n\nView > Preview (Shift+P) opens a small window showing the design at the size it will have in the game, at 1 to 4 times, over a checkerboard or a plain background.\n\n### Copy, paste and the library\n\nCopy, cut, paste and duplicate (Ctrl/Cmd+C, X, V, D) work on shapes, with their points and groups. Pasting works between designs. Edit > Save selection to library keeps the selection for good, and Edit > Library inserts it into any design.\n\n## Saving your work\n\n- **Browser storage.** Every change is saved as you make it. The browser may delete this storage when you clear site data.\n- **A file on disk.** In Chrome and Edge, File > Save to file (Ctrl/Cmd+S) asks for a `.design.json` file, and from then on rewrites it after every change. File > Open file (or Open file on the start screen) reopens it. The browser asks for permission again when you return to a design, and clicking the file name in the status bar grants it. Other browsers download the file instead.\n- **Version history.** A snapshot is taken when you open a design, every five minutes while you work, and when you choose File > Save version. File > Version history lists them and restores one. Restoring can be undone.\n- **JSON.** **Export JSON** (a button at the top, next to Export GML, and in the File menu) saves a `.design.json` file and **Import** adds one as a new design.\n- **SVG.** **Import** also accepts an `.svg` file, and File > Import SVG adds one to the open design. See Importing SVG below.\n\n## Importing SVG\n\nChoose **Import** on the start screen to turn an `.svg` file into a new design, or File > Import SVG to add one to the open design. A window shows what the import will make before anything is added, and lists what does not carry over.\n\nOptions in the window:\n\n- **Scale** multiplies every size. SVG files are often drawn much larger than a game sprite.\n- **Curve detail** sets how far a straight piece may stray from the original curve: Fine is 0.1 px, Normal 0.25 px, Coarse 0.5 px and Very coarse 1 px. Fewer points draw faster in the game.\n- The window also shows the number of shapes and points, and about how many triangles the result costs when exported.\n\nThe middle of the file's artboard (its `viewBox`) becomes the design's origin. Use Origin in Properties to move it.\n\nWhat is imported:\n\n- Paths, rectangles (with rounded corners), circles, ellipses, lines, polylines and polygons, including transforms, `use` and `symbol`, and style sheets.\n- Curves and arcs, cut into short straight pieces. A circle or ellipse stays a true circle, and a rectangle stays a rectangle.\n- Shapes with holes, following the fill rule. A path made of several separate pieces becomes one combined shape.\n- Solid fills, opacity, and linear and radial gradients with any number of stops.\n- Strokes. Each stroke becomes its own line shape on top of its fill, centered on the edge as in SVG, with round or flat ends and the stroke color, width and opacity.\n- Top-level groups become groups. The layer that wraps a whole file is not made a group.\n\nWhat does not carry over, and is listed in the window when the file uses it:\n\n- Filters (blur, shadows, glow), clipping paths and masks, blend modes, markers, patterns, embedded images, embedded web content, and text. Convert text to outlines in your drawing app before exporting.\n- Dashed lines are drawn solid, square line ends are drawn flat, and stroke corners are always round.\n- A gradient stop with transparency is drawn solid, a gradient that repeats or reflects is drawn once, a radial gradient is always round and centered on its shape, and a gradient on a stroke is drawn in its first color.\n- Transparency on a group is applied to each shape in it separately, which looks different where shapes overlap.\n- Groups inside groups are merged into the outer group.\n\nImported shapes keep their exact points. They are not snapped to the pixel grid, and resizing one does not snap them either. After an import, tidy the shapes in the editor, and use Export GML to see what the design costs.\n\n## Exporting an image\n\n**Export image** (the button at the top, or File > Export image) saves the design as a PNG or an SVG picture, for sharing, for a store page, or for a sprite you want to draw in another tool. The picture is drawn the same way the canvas draws it, so gradients, outlines, opacity, lines and curves all appear. Hidden shapes and reference images are left out. A preview shows the result.\n\n- **Size** is 1\u00d7, 2\u00d7, 3\u00d7, 4\u00d7 or 8\u00d7 the design's pixels. The edges of the picture are on whole pixels, so a 1\u00d7 PNG matches the design pixel for pixel.\n- **Padding** adds space around the drawing, in pixels.\n- **Bounds** is either fitted to the drawing, or centered on the origin so that the origin is the middle of the picture, which makes it easy to line the picture up in a game.\n- **Curves** is Smooth (128 sides on every circle) or the same number of sides the game uses, which shows what the game draws.\n- **Background** is transparent unless you choose a color.\n- **Only the selected shapes** exports just what you have selected.\n\nAn SVG made this way keeps circles and curves as straight pieces, with as many sides as you chose. The PNG is limited to 8192 pixels on its longest side.\n\n## Exporting GML\n\n**Export GML** (File menu, or the button at the top right) opens a window with the generated script, a cost summary and four settings.\n\n- **Name prefix** is added to the start of every function name. The default is `design_`.\n- **Naming style** is snake_case, camelCase or PascalCase. It applies to function names and to your shape and point names.\n- **Circle sides** sets how many sides every circle has, and how finely rounded corners and outlines are cut. More sides are smoother and cost more. It starts at the design's own Circle sides setting.\n- **Gradient smoothness** is the default for how finely a radial gradient is split to follow its blend, from 1 to 16. More is smoother and costs more. A shape with its own smoothness (see Color) keeps it. The default is 4.\n\nCircle sides and Gradient smoothness apply to that export only. The design keeps its own circle sides.\n\nThe cost summary updates as you change the settings. It shows how many triangles one draw of the design sends, for the exact script in the window, and a bar for each of the heaviest shapes with the reason, such as a radial gradient or an outline. Enter a number of copies to see the total for a frame. A short list of tips appears when one shape dominates the cost. The Light, Moderate and Heavy labels are rules of thumb and are not timings, because the script draws with GML code every frame. Test on your slowest target. A design that never changes can be drawn once into a sprite with the bake function.\n\nUse **Copy code** or **Download .gml**. Create a script asset in GameMaker and paste the code into it. Each design is a single script and needs no other files. The settings are remembered between exports, and the first line of the script records the export date.\n\nNames are converted to valid GML identifiers. Spaces and symbols are removed, names that match GML keywords get a trailing underscore, and duplicates get a number.\n\n## Using a design in GameMaker\n\nThe examples use a design called `player` with the default settings, so its functions start with `design_player_`. Your functions follow your own design name and prefix. The shape and point names `head`, `body` and `hover` stand in for yours. The script uses structs and optional arguments, so it needs a current version of GameMaker.\n\n### Basic use\n\nMake a copy of the design, give it a name, and draw it by that name.\n\n```gml\n// Create event\ndesign_player_create(\"hero\");\n\n// Draw event\ndesign_player_draw(\"hero\", x, y);\n\n// Clean Up event\ndesign_player_destroy(\"hero\");\n```\n\nEach copy is independent of the others. Names belong to a single design, so `\"hero\"` in one design's script is unrelated to `\"hero\"` in another's.\n\n### Several objects\n\nEvery copy needs its own name. Building the name from the instance id works for any number of objects.\n\n```gml\n// Create event\nmy_design = \"player_\" + string(id);\ndesign_player_create(my_design);\n\n// Draw event\ndesign_player_draw(my_design, x, y);\n\n// Clean Up event\ndesign_player_destroy(my_design);\n```\n\n`design_player_exists(name)` returns whether a name is in use.\n\n### Changing a copy\n\n`design_player_get(name)` returns the data behind a copy. Changes show the next time it is drawn.\n\n```gml\nvar d = design_player_get(\"hero\");\n\nd.shapes.head.x += 2;             // slide a shape\nd.shapes.head.angle += 5;         // turn it\nd.shapes.head.xscale = 1.5;       // stretch it\nd.shapes.head.color = c_red;      // recolor it\nd.shapes.head.alpha = 0.5;        // make it translucent\nd.shapes.head.visible = false;    // hide it\n\nd.shapes.body.x = d.points.hover.x;   // move a shape to a point\nd.shapes.body.y = d.points.hover.y;\n```\n\nEvery shape has these fields.\n\n- `x`, `y`: position in the design, in pixels from the design origin. +y is down. This is the shape's pivot, which is its center unless you moved it.\n- `angle`: rotation in degrees, counter-clockwise.\n- `xscale`, `yscale`: stretch. 1 is the designed size and -1 mirrors the shape.\n- `color`: a GameMaker color.\n- `alpha`: 0 to 1.\n- `visible`: true or false.\n- `fill_type`: `\"solid\"`, `\"linear\"` or `\"radial\"`. A gradient blends from `color` to `color2`.\n- `color2`: the far color of a gradient.\n- `gradient_angle`: the direction of a linear gradient in degrees. 0 is left to right and 90 is top to bottom. It turns with the shape.\n- `gradient_detail`: for radial gradients, how finely each triangle is split to follow the blend. Raise it for smoother results. It starts at the shape's own smoothness, or at the export window's Gradient smoothness when the shape has none.\n- `gradient_stops`: the extra colors between `color` and `color2`, as `[[position, color], ...]` with positions from 0 to 1. Changing `color` or `color2` at runtime keeps the stops where they are.\n- `gradient_box`: the area the gradient spans, as `[left, top, right, bottom]` from the shape's pivot.\n- `outline`: the outline width in pixels, 0 for none. It grows outward from the edge, with round corners.\n- `outline_color`: a GameMaker color.\n- `line_width`, `line_cap`: on a line only. The thickness in pixels, and `\"round\"` or `\"flat\"` ends. A line is drawn in `color`, and its `parts` hold one part of kind `\"line\"` with its points in `corners`.\n- `swatch`, `outline_swatch`: the name of the palette color the fill or outline follows, or an empty string.\n- `points`: the shape's own points, each with `x` and `y` as an offset from the shape's pivot (its center if it has none).\n- `parts`: the geometry. Polygons hold `corners` and `triangles`, and ellipses hold `rx` and `ry`. Changing a corner reshapes the polygon.\n\n`design.shapes` holds the shapes by name, `design.points` holds the design points by name, and `design.order` lists the shapes from back to front. Reordering that array changes what draws on top. `design.circle_segments` is the number of sides each circle has. It starts at the design's Circle sides setting, and changing it changes every circle in that copy.\n\n`design.groups` holds each group as an array of its shapes, by group name, so a whole group can be changed in a loop.\n\n```gml\nvar arm = design_player_get(\"hero\").groups.arm;\nfor (var i = 0; i < array_length(arm); i++) arm[i].alpha = 0.5;\n```\n\n### Colors\n\nPalette colors are stored in `design.palette`, by name. Changing one with `design_player_set_swatch` also updates every shape that uses it, outlines included. A shape's own `color` can still be set directly. Do that to a shape that uses a palette color and the next `set_swatch` call for that color overwrites it.\n\n```gml\ndesign_player_create(\"red_team\");\ndesign_player_create(\"blue_team\");\ndesign_player_set_swatch(\"blue_team\", \"shirt\", c_blue);\n```\n\n`design.palette.shirt` reads the current color. The names are the ones used in the editor, converted to the naming style you chose.\n\n### Changing one shape's color, and flashing\n\n`design_player_set_color` changes one shape, a whole group, or a list of shapes:\n\n```gml\ndesign_player_set_color(\"hero\", \"shirt\", c_red);          // one shape\ndesign_player_set_color(\"hero\", \"arm\", c_blue);           // every shape in the group \"arm\"\ndesign_player_set_color(\"hero\", \"glow\", c_yellow, c_red); // a gradient: first and last color\n```\n\nThe shape stops following its palette color. To make a second version of a design in other colors, palette colors and `set_swatch` are still the better tool.\n\n`design_player_set_flash` mixes every color of the design towards one color, for a hit flash. The shapes keep their own colors underneath:\n\n```gml\ndesign_player_set_flash(\"hero\", c_white, 1);   // flash\ndesign_player_set_flash(\"hero\", c_white, 0);   // back to normal (fade it by lowering the amount each step)\n```\n\nA flash is cheap with `draw`. A baked design has to bake again each time the flash changes, so use `draw` for things that flash.\n\n### Points\n\nA design point is read directly, as `d.points.hover`. A point on a shape depends on where the shape is, so ask for it by name.\n\n```gml\nvar spot = design_player_shape_point(\"hero\", \"head\", \"eye\");   // in design space\nvar where = design_player_to_room(spot, x, y);                 // in the room\n// where.x, where.y\n```\n\n`design_player_to_room` takes the same x, y, scale and angle you pass to the draw call.\n\nBecause a shape's `x` and `y` are its pivot, you can swing a shape from a joint and have things follow it. This turns an arm around its shoulder and puts an item in the hand:\n\n```gml\nvar d = design_player_get(\"hero\");\nd.shapes.arm.angle = 20 * dsin(current_time * 0.2);              // swing around the pivot\nvar hand = design_player_shape_point(\"hero\", \"arm\", \"hand\");     // follows the turn\nvar spot = design_player_to_room(hand, x, y);\nsword_x = spot.x; sword_y = spot.y;\n```\n\nTo attach one shape to another, copy a point of the first onto the second: `d.shapes.sword.x = hand.x; d.shapes.sword.y = hand.y;`. A shape that is attached this way turns around its own pivot, so put its pivot at the grip.\n\n### Drawing\n\n`design_player_draw` takes optional arguments after the position, in the style of `draw_sprite_ext`.\n\n```gml\ndesign_player_draw(\"hero\", x, y, xscale, yscale, angle, blend, alpha);\n```\n\n`blend` tints the design by multiplying each color, as `image_blend` does. White leaves the colors unchanged.\n\nFor many copies that no longer change, draw once into a sprite and draw the sprite.\n\n```gml\n// Draw event, once\nsprite = design_player_bake(\"hero\");\n\n// Draw event, every frame\ndraw_sprite(sprite, 0, x, y);\n\n// when finished\nsprite_delete(sprite);\n```\n\nMost of the time `design_player_draw_baked` is easier, because it makes the sprite the first time and keeps it:\n\n```gml\n// Draw event, every frame\ndesign_player_draw_baked(\"scenery\", x, y);\n\n// after you change the design (move a shape, hide one...)\ndesign_player_clear_baked(\"scenery\");\n```\n\n`design_player_draw_baked` takes the same arguments as `draw`, and the position, scale, angle, blend and alpha still work, because they are applied when the sprite is drawn. It costs one sprite instead of every triangle, which is a big saving for scenery and for anything that does not animate. Set `design.bake_scale` to 2 or more before the first draw for a sharper sprite when it will be drawn large. `set_color`, `set_swatch` and `set_flash` clear the saved sprite for you, so the next draw makes a new one. If it is called while drawing into a surface, it draws the design normally that time, because a surface cannot be made inside another.\n\n`design_player_bake` renders through a surface, so call it from a Draw event. The sprite origin is the design origin, so `draw_sprite(sprite, 0, x, y)` lands where the draw function would. An optional second argument adds padding in pixels, and a third makes the sprite that many times bigger than the design. A baked sprite does not change when the design does.\n\n### Using a struct instead of a name\n\n`design_player_create()` with no name returns the copy itself, and every function accepts it in place of a name.\n\n```gml\nd = design_player_create();\ndesign_player_draw(d, x, y);\n```\n\nGameMaker frees the struct when nothing refers to it, so there is nothing to destroy. Assigning one struct to another variable does not copy it. Call create again for a new copy.\n\n### Function reference\n\n- `design_player_create([name])`: makes a copy and returns it. With a name, the copy can also be found by that name.\n- `design_player_get(design)`: returns the struct for a name or struct.\n- `design_player_exists(name)`: returns true if the name is in use.\n- `design_player_destroy(design)`: removes a named copy so the name can be reused.\n- `design_player_draw(design, [x], [y], [xscale], [yscale], [angle], [blend], [alpha])`: draws the copy.\n- `design_player_bake(design, [padding], [scale])`: renders the copy into a new sprite and returns it.\n- `design_player_draw_baked(design, [x], [y], [xscale], [yscale], [angle], [blend], [alpha])`: draws the copy from a saved sprite, made the first time.\n- `design_player_clear_baked(design)`: throws the saved sprite away, so the next `draw_baked` makes a new one.\n- `design_player_get_bounds(design, [x], [y], [xscale], [yscale], [angle])`: returns the box around everything visible, as a struct with `left`, `top`, `right` and `bottom`.\n- `design_player_shape_point(design, shape, point_name)`: returns a shape point as `{ x, y }` in design space.\n- `design_player_to_room(point, [x], [y], [xscale], [yscale], [angle])`: converts a design-space point to room coordinates.\n- `design_player_set_swatch(design, name, color)`: changes a palette color, and every shape that uses it.\n- `design_player_set_color(design, target, color, [color2])`: changes the color of a shape, a group or a list of shapes.\n- `design_player_set_flash(design, color, amount)`: mixes every color towards one color (a hit flash). 0 is off.\n\n`design` is a name or a struct. `shape` is a name or a shape struct. All draw and conversion arguments after the first default to no change.\n\n### Conventions\n\n- Units are pixels, +y points down, and the design origin is (0, 0).\n- Angles turn counter-clockwise, like `image_angle`. The editor rotates clockwise, and the exported numbers are converted.\n- Colors are exported in GameMaker's blue-green-red order.\n- Polygons are cut into triangles when the script is generated. Circles are polygons with `design.circle_segments` sides, drawn as a fan of triangles.\n\n### Errors\n\nThe game stops with a message in these cases.\n\n- A function is given a name that has not been created.\n- `design_player_create` is given a name that is already in use.\n- `design_player_shape_point` is given a shape or point name that does not exist.\n- `design_player_set_swatch` is given a palette name that does not exist.\n\n## Notes\n\n- Exported scripts are generated. Make changes in the editor and export again instead of editing the script, unless the change is meant to be permanent.\n- A radial gradient is approximated by cutting each triangle into smaller ones, so it can show faint steps on large shapes. Raise `gradient_detail` if it does. A linear gradient is exact, including its stops, because every triangle is cut where the gradient reaches a stop. An outline sits under the fill, so a shape with an outline and an opacity below 100% shows the outline through the fill. Whole-design transparency is the `alpha` argument of the draw function.\n- A line with an opacity below 100% can look slightly darker where its bends overlap, because the pieces are drawn separately.\n- Outlines are drawn as a band along each edge with a round wedge at each outside corner, so an outline looks the same in the game as in the editor. A rounded or smoothed shape is exported as ordinary corners, so those corners are already worked out when the script runs.\n- Collision shapes and animation are not part of the editor.\n\n## Files\n\n- `index.html`: the page.\n- `studio.css`: styling.\n- `core.js`: helpers, storage and shape geometry.\n- `boolean.js`: union, subtract, intersect and exclude.\n- `curves.js`: rounded corners, smooth lines and B\u00e9zier curves.\n- `linestyle.js`: dashed, tapered and arrowed lines.\n- `studio.js`: the design and every operation on it.\n- `files.js`: version history and the linked file.\n- `ui.js`: menus, toolbar, panels and shortcuts.\n- `canvas.js`: drawing and mouse handling.\n- `gml.js`: the GML exporter and its cost summary.\n- `svg.js`: SVG import.\n- `reference.js`: reference images.\n- `pattern.js`: copies in a pattern.\n- `image.js`: PNG and SVG export.\n- `main.js`: start-up.\n- `docs.js` and `docs-content.js`: this documentation as shown in the app.\n\n## License\n\nMIT. See `LICENSE`.\n";
